 # Diagnosing Bugs
@@ -111,6 +111,17 @@ Tool preference:
 
 **Perf branch.** For performance regressions, logs are usually wrong. Instead: establish a baseline measurement (timing harness, `performance.now()`, profiler, query plan), then bisect. Measure first, fix second.
 
+**Hillclimb.** When the goal is to push one measurable thing (latency, memory, bundle size, test time) toward a target rather than fix one regression, loop instead of bisecting:
+
+1. Fix one metric, the direction that counts as better, and a stop predicate that pairs a target with a minimum number of attempts, so a lucky early win cannot end the run ("≥30% below baseline and ≥5 attempts").
+2. Freeze the harness before changing code: one command that prints the metric as the median of N runs, plus the count of work done and errors, run on a workload that reproduces the complaint. Record the baseline and a green run of the regression tests.
+3. One hypothesis per attempt, each naming a mechanism ("defer X off the startup path because it blocks first paint"), not "try memoizing something". Measure before and after with the frozen harness and run the tests.
+4. Keep a change only when the metric moves past noise and the tests stay green; otherwise revert it in full. Never stack untested changes, never claim a win from reading code. One commit per kept change.
+5. Log every attempt, kept or reverted (hypothesis, before, after, verdict) in an untracked file, and read it before the next attempt.
+6. On a stall, change category or re-read the source before declaring the hill climbed. Stop at the predicate or when the remaining ideas cost more than they would gain; never relax the predicate to meet it.
+
+Report the baseline, the final value with the percent change, attempts kept and reverted, and one line per kept change.
+
 ## Phase 5: Fix + regression test
 
 Write the regression test **before the fix**, but only if there is a **correct seam** for it.
