+### When fixes keep failing: attack the premise
+
+If two fixes that rest on the same premise have failed the same check, suspect the premise, not the fixes. Each failure is evidence against the belief both fixes assumed.
+
+1. Write the premise as one sentence that every failed fix assumed.
+2. Before the next fix, make an observation that would come out differently if the premise were false, and make it rerunnable. Look at how the failures are distributed, not only how large they are.
+3. If the observation contradicts the premise, go back to Phase 3 with what the failures share that the premise did not predict. If it confirms the premise, ask the next "why": the earlier fixes may have treated a consequence.
+4. Remove the condition that causes the failure instead of adding a mechanism that cleans up after it.
+
+Do not write a third fix on an untested premise.
+
